{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/1512908-chimerical-death-in-the-dreaming-question","id":1512908,"title":"Chimerical Death In The Dreaming Question","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Changeling: The Dreaming"],"posts":[{"date":"2023-05-26T13:55:47-07:00","id":1512908,"title":"Chimerical Death In The Dreaming Question","content":"I was looking through the book, and I see two different rules for dying while in the Dreaming.<br /> <br /> On Page 291:<br /> <br /> Chimerical death occurs when the changeling suffers more<br /> lethal or aggravated chimerical damage then she has chimerical<br /> health levels. The changeling passes out immediately, and<br /> when she awakens she suffers from the Mists, all memories of<br /> her fae life inaccessible. At best she remembers them as a dream,<br /> or perhaps a nightmare. Her Fae nature can be restored after<br /> a certain amount of time, depending on her Banality rating<br /> (see the Mists chart on p. 269), with an infusion of Glamour.<br /> Those who suffer a chimerical death while in the Dreaming<br /> are expelled from it, waking up in the Autumn world with no<br /> memory of how they got there.\u200b<br /> <br /> And then on Page 300:<br /> <br /> The Dreaming makes no distinction between chimerical<br /> damage (and death) and physical death. A changeling that<br /> suffers either form of death in the Dreaming dies, their soul<br /> passing on to a new incarnation (or, in the case of Arcadian<br /> sidhe, disappearing for parts unknown). For someone with an<br /> anchor in the Autumn world, death means that the physical<br /> body falls into a coma and wastes away.\u200b<br /> <br /> <br /> Has there ever been a clarification on which one is the right rules, or am I misunderstanding something?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7550-vulcan7200","name":"Vulcan7200","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"71"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-02T13:54:29-07:00","id":1513705,"title":"","content":"I do not have a canonical answer, but this is how I think of it.<br /> <br /> I am not familiar with C20, but having looked at the second edition corebook, it did not make the distinction found here. There's no mention of what happens to the physical body in the Dreaming after chimerical death. And the only way for true death to occur (the fae part is permanently over and does not reincarnate) is with cold iron.<br /> <br /> So it's possible these sections were written by two different writers, each trying to address an issue.<br /> <br /> The first issue is what happens to the physical mortal body if the changeling suffers chimerical death in the Dreaming. It wasn't addressed in 2e, and it is probably an issue many players had happen in their chronicles. So I think that the description tries to answer that - it gets expelled, and use the rules normally from then. Sounds good to me.<br /> <br /> The second issue seems less like a problem players needed fixing, and more like what a certain writer felt the setting should be like. Instead of limiting true death to injuries from cold iron, and wanting more of a real fear about character death, this part got tacked in. I don't think that was the original intent of the game. So what should you do as ST? Well, whatever you think is best for your game and your conception of the setting. If you only want to go with cold iron permanently killing fae, then ignore page 300. If you want to increase the danger of your chronicle or think that such a threat for changelings in the Dreaming is a good thing for your setting (perhaps to &quot;force&quot; them to stay in the Autumn World, than incorporate page 300.<br /> <br /> As for me, I probably would not go with the rules in page 300. It makes the Dreaming incredibly hostile to Changelings (because creatures from the Dreaming will almost always do chimerical damage), which I think defeats the main theme of the game. The Autumn World ironically becomes safer for Changelings than the Dreaming because death in the Autumn World won't prevent reincarnation of the fae soul, but death in the Dreaming will likely prevent that. I think it is a much stronger theme that the Dreaming - being the native realm of the fae - should be more hospitable, and the Autumn World is more dangerous. There are still plenty of threats in the Dreaming (like Bedlam) that prevent the Dreaming from being completely safe and encourage changelings to interact with the Autumn World.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-10T13:12:44-07:00","id":1514500,"title":"","content":"I agree with <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar3046_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"3046\" data-vbnamecard=\"3046\">Black Fox</a> it makes little sense for the source of glamour and the Fey to be a place of final death.<br /> <br /> I\u2019d do the same that death in the Dreaming can be overcome by a powerful investment of glamour, but Chimeric death in the Banal autumn world is final Fey death and destruction of the feys reincarnated soul.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27824-arcanus","name":"Arcanus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27824_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"148"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}